00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:06.130
Now that we have discussed the various steppe
polities of the early medieval Period and

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:10.980
the way they were established and destroyed
we can discuss more closely the life of the

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:13.760
steppe “nomads” that lived in these polities.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:18.570
When people picture the history of the Eurasian
steppe they often picture an endless sea of

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:24.220
grasslands filled with nomads living in yurts
that sporadically follow their animal herds

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:26.340
wherever they happen to migrate.

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:31.370
These nomads then intermittently formed into
bigger political groups and marched into the

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:35.149
civilized world where they caused chaos and
destruction.

00:08:35.149 --> 00:08:40.370
This picture, however, is filled with stereotypes
and isn’t very accurate.

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:45.230
The nomads of the Eurasian steppe could build
up or destroy powerful empires, they could

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:50.689
form complex social hierarchies and even build
cities the splendour of which impressed their

00:08:50.689 --> 00:08:52.420
local “sedentary” counterparts.

00:08:52.420 --> 00:08:57.939
None of this could be done by a society which
solely centred around herding and migrating.

00:08:57.939 --> 00:09:02.860
The early medieval nomadic societies of the
Euroasian steppe were far more complex than

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:04.809
is often talked about.

00:09:04.809 --> 00:09:08.940
Probably the most important thing to mention
here, is that the Eurasian Steppe in the early

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:13.139
medieval period did have urban centres or
at least settlements.

00:09:13.139 --> 00:09:18.410
Of course, these were on average smaller than
the urban centres of China or Persia and there

00:09:18.410 --> 00:09:20.509
were less of them, but they did exist.

00:09:20.509 --> 00:09:25.870
The Euroasian steppe was not some kind of
a sea of neverending grasslands with no farming

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:27.959
or settlements or urban centres.

00:09:27.959 --> 00:09:32.610
The trading cities on the Syr Darya river
are well documented, as are the Khazar trading

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:37.910
cities on the Volga and Don, and the Uyghur
capital was said to have been so magnificent

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:41.210
it could rival any city in the “urbanized”
world.

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:45.279
Even in today’s northern Kazakhstan, an
area believed to have been devoid of any urbanization,

00:09:45.279 --> 00:09:51.629
a 9th century Arab explorer Tamim ibn Bahr
wrote of Kimak “villages and cultivated

00:09:51.629 --> 00:09:54.029
tracts of land” around the Irtysh river.

00:09:54.029 --> 00:09:58.889
As you can see the early medieval Euroasian
steppe contained settled people who farmed

00:09:58.889 --> 00:10:03.600
and also urban centres that produced goods,
materials and traded with the rest of the

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:04.600
world.

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:08.740
Yes, there most certainly were a majority
of nomads in the steppe that did live a life

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:13.930
dictated by sporadic seasonal migrations of
their herds, but this was by far not the only

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:18.709
lifestyle present in the steppe nor was this
lifestyle static for the people that lived

00:10:18.709 --> 00:10:19.709
it.

00:10:19.709 --> 00:10:22.600
By that I mean the nomads weren’t always
nomadic.

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:27.860
People who lived a nomadic life in the steppe
could and did embrace a sedentary lifestyle

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:33.040
either by choice or when social, economic,
and political pressures forced them into it.

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:37.560
For example, the Khazars found themselves
in control of the extremely valuable Volga

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:42.519
and Don river trade routes and to consolidate
control over this trade they constructed and

00:10:42.519 --> 00:10:44.699
settled in cities along the rivers.

00:10:44.699 --> 00:10:49.910
The Khazars were nomads who, forced by political
and economic pressures, had to consolidate

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:53.420
power by becoming, at least in part, sedentary.

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:58.129
This is not to say that all Khazars became
sedentary, for example the Khazar ruling class

00:10:58.129 --> 00:11:03.029
was said to have only wintered in their capital
of Atil, and there were Khazar people that

00:11:03.029 --> 00:11:08.139
did continue on herding in the Southern Russia
Steppe, but it does go to show that nomads

00:11:08.139 --> 00:11:11.740
could and did sometimes embrace sedentary
lifestyles.

00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:16.189
In contrast to that, settled people in the
steppe, who might have been former nomads

00:11:16.189 --> 00:11:21.499
themselves, could also abandon a settled or
an urban life and become nomads again.

00:11:21.499 --> 00:11:27.270
This happened when a Gokturk Khagan of the
Second Turkic Khaganate attacked the Uyghurs.

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:32.980
He stated that “I marched down along the
Selenga river and there laid waste to their

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:34.000
[Uyghur] settlements.”

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:38.589
further stating that Uyghurs dispersed mostly
east but also west.

00:11:38.589 --> 00:11:43.410
The Uyghur settlements were destroyed and
so the Uyghurs had to go back to a nomadic

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:48.300
life in the steppe, biding their time and
finding new allies before attacking the Gokturks

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:51.910
and destroying their Khaganate as we already
talked about.

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:56.500
Hopefully with these examples it has become
clear that a nomadic steppe life wasn’t

00:11:56.500 --> 00:12:02.379
stagnant, it could involve months, years or
even generations of sedentary existence before

00:12:02.379 --> 00:12:05.730
reverting back to nomadic herding, or vice
versa.

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:10.480
The nomads of the Euroasian steppe were not
foreign to the idea of farming or settling

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:11.480
in an urban centre.

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:17.199
They were also aware that successfully running
a polity required some sort of centralized

00:12:17.199 --> 00:12:19.439
leadership, administration, and management.

00:12:19.439 --> 00:12:24.959
This is why when new nomadic polities came
to be, like that of the Khazar or Uyghur Khaganate,

00:12:24.959 --> 00:12:29.050
the nomads knew they had to construct cities
and so they did.

00:12:29.050 --> 00:12:33.999
However, this centralization, urbanization,
and settlement was not always successful or

00:12:33.999 --> 00:12:38.660
welcomed by some members of the nomadic communities
within newly formed polities.

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:43.119
For example there was a very prominent Gokturk
general in the Second Turkic Khaganate, called

00:12:43.119 --> 00:12:49.399
Tonyukuk, who vehemently believed that “the
only way for them (the nomadic Turks) to maintain

00:12:49.399 --> 00:12:53.970
their national identity was ‘to follow the
water and the grass’ (meaning a nomadic

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:56.269
lifestyle) and have no permanent dwellings.

00:12:56.269 --> 00:13:01.199
If the Turks were to change their old customs,
he argued, one day they would be defeated

00:13:01.199 --> 00:13:02.959
and annexed by the Chinese.”

00:13:02.959 --> 00:13:08.339
Thanks to people like Tonyukuk we know that
the idea of a nomadic life vs a settled life

00:13:08.339 --> 00:13:10.970
was a subject of debate in nomadic communities.

00:13:10.970 --> 00:13:15.369
Therefore, as with almost everything, the
nomadic life of the Eurasian steppe was a

00:13:15.369 --> 00:13:20.730
spectrum with people on one side arguing for
a settled life and urban centres and people

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:22.839
on the other for a nomadic life.

00:13:22.839 --> 00:13:26.949
Most nomads most likely fell somewhere in
between these two extremes.

00:13:26.949 --> 00:13:32.050
Which is why we see a variety of lifestyles
in the Eurasian steppe, and why a nomadic

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:37.119
life was fluid and could contain nomads settling
in cities and then also due to a variety of

00:13:37.119 --> 00:13:40.850
reasons abandoning these cities and becoming
nomads again.

00:13:40.850 --> 00:13:46.619
Another aspect of the Euroasian steppe nomads
that is often misrepresented is their autarky,

00:13:46.619 --> 00:13:49.029
in other words their self-sufficiency.

00:13:49.029 --> 00:13:52.199
The nomads of the Eurasian steppe were not
at all autarkic.

00:13:52.199 --> 00:13:57.920
On the contrary, they needed a steady stream
of goods like pottery or silk and raw materials

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:01.250
like iron or wood from sedentary societies.

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:06.050
The nomads simply just couldn’t get hold
of, or produce, all these goods in big enough

00:14:06.050 --> 00:14:09.800
quantities and therefore relied on sedentary
societies.

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:13.999
There were several ways the nomads acquired
these goods and materials from these sedentary

00:14:13.999 --> 00:14:15.050
societies.

00:14:15.050 --> 00:14:20.660
First was from the urban centres that already
existed in the Eurasian steppe, in fact one

00:14:20.660 --> 00:14:25.980
of the theories for why there were settlements
in the Eurasian steppe is that they were created

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:27.940
to fulfill an economic need.

00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:30.100
This is, however, still debated.

00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:34.839
Second way of getting these goods and materials
was through just trading, this is partly why

00:14:34.839 --> 00:14:40.069
nomads were often so keen on exploiting trade
routes like the Khazars did and why they often

00:14:40.069 --> 00:14:43.839
tried to conquer areas significant for trading,
like the Silk Road.

00:14:43.839 --> 00:14:48.829
Third was raiding urban centres, or through
coercion of tribute payments of goods and

00:14:48.829 --> 00:14:53.259
materials to not raid these urban centres,
pretty self explanatory.

00:14:53.259 --> 00:14:57.819
Fourth was conquering sedentary societies
and then exploiting them, kind of like the

00:14:57.819 --> 00:15:01.079
Avars did with the Slavs, or the Uyghurs with
the Sogdians.

00:15:01.079 --> 00:15:06.389
Lastly, fifth, was nomads building their own
urban centres and producing their own goods

00:15:06.389 --> 00:15:07.639
and materials.

00:15:07.639 --> 00:15:12.980
Most notable example of this was the Uyghur
capital of Ordu-Baliq which was reported by

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:19.040
arab explorers in the 9th century as being
“a great city, rich in agriculture and surrounded

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:23.139
by districts full of cultivation and villages
lying close together.

00:15:23.139 --> 00:15:26.439
The city has twelve iron gates of huge size.

00:15:26.439 --> 00:15:31.769
The city is also populous and thickly crowded
and has markets and various traders.”

00:15:31.769 --> 00:15:36.290
Through archaeology we can also gleam that
the city had sizable hinterlands which focused

00:15:36.290 --> 00:15:40.769
on production of goods most certainly to fulfil
the needs of the nomadic polity.

00:15:40.769 --> 00:15:46.160
Of course, none of these five ways of acquiring
goods and materials were mutually exclusive

00:15:46.160 --> 00:15:51.190
and all these solutions were used interchangeably
by various nomadic groups.

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:55.730
With all that said, there’s a very good
reason why when it comes to the Euroasian

00:15:55.730 --> 00:16:01.600
steppe nomads the “nomadic” aspect of
their life is often overemphasized by people

00:16:01.600 --> 00:16:03.540
talking about their history.

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:08.829
This mostly happens because the nomadic life
is what made the nomads so unique and it is

00:16:08.829 --> 00:16:13.119
the thing that gave them the edge over their
sedentary counterparts.

00:16:13.119 --> 00:16:18.889
Their lifestyle was the reason why the Euroasian
nomads were time and time again so influential

00:16:18.889 --> 00:16:20.569
in Euroasian history.

00:16:20.569 --> 00:16:25.790
The nomadic lifestyle forced the people of
the steppe to become very good at horse riding,

00:16:25.790 --> 00:16:29.410
archery, and be self-sufficient for long periods
of time.

00:16:29.410 --> 00:16:33.839
As discussed they still needed goods and materials
from sedentary people but they could live

00:16:33.839 --> 00:16:38.290
without these things for far longer than any
other sedentary people could.

00:16:38.290 --> 00:16:43.000
All these skills which the nomads learned
in the steppe coincidentally also made them

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:45.170
skilled and efficient warriors.

00:16:45.170 --> 00:16:50.569
They were perfect soldiers for military campaigns
as they could mobilize and travers great distances

00:16:50.569 --> 00:16:52.329
in a short amount of time.

00:16:52.329 --> 00:16:56.959
On top of that, the fact that a nomadic lifestyle
required the involvement of everyone in the

00:16:56.959 --> 00:17:01.670
nomadic society meant that despite smaller
population numbers the nomads of the Euroasian

00:17:01.670 --> 00:17:07.650
steppe could field just as large armies as
their much more populous sedentary neighbors.

00:17:07.650 --> 00:17:11.740
Almost everyone in the nomadic society could
be a skilled warrior if needed.

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:17.370
Therefore, it was the nomadic lifestyle that
empowered the Euroasian nomads to be so influential

00:17:17.370 --> 00:17:22.750
in Euroasian history and that is why their
“nomadic” life is often emphasized in

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:29.060
most literature about them which, however,
overshadows the more nuanced reality.

00:17:29.060 --> 00:17:34.230
The reality being that most early medieval
Euroasian nomads did live a nomadic life which

00:17:34.230 --> 00:17:40.910
however, was not self-sufficient, not stagnant,
and not devoid of farming or urban centres

00:17:40.910 --> 00:17:42.420
as is often thought.

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:47.930
Just as any people the nomads were pragmatic
and when needed they could settle and farm

00:17:47.930 --> 00:18:19.460
or build cities to centralize power or abandon
all this and start herding again.

